\title{Diagnosing Julia-Based Fits in CoTiMA2:\\
  A Systematic Workflow for Speed, Convergence, and Identification}
\author{C. Dormann, \& $\widehat{\textnormal{C}}$. G. Petee}
\date{CoTiMA2 0.14.59 --- 6 November 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

<<setup, include=FALSE, purl=FALSE>>=
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  echo = TRUE, eval = FALSE, purl = FALSE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE,
  comment = "#>", tidy = FALSE, size = "small"
)
options(width = 92)
library(CoTiMA2)
@

\begin{abstract}
This guide provides a reproducible diagnostic workflow for maximum-likelihood
models fitted with the Julia backend of CoTiMA2. It separates computational
problems from optimization and statistical-identification problems. It also
explains why a small improvement in the objective, a non-zero raw gradient,
or a large change in an unconstrained parameter need not have the same
interpretation. The examples are templates: replace object names and model
arguments with those used in the analysis being diagnosed.
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\section{The three questions that should not be mixed}

A slow or non-converged fit can arise for very different reasons:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Computational:} Julia parallelism is unavailable, unsafe, or
    configured inefficiently.
  \item \textbf{Numerical:} the optimizer uses poor starting values, an
    unsuitable step size, or a difficult raw parameter scale.
  \item \textbf{Statistical:} some parameters are weakly identified, close to
    a boundary, or lie on a nearly flat likelihood ridge.
\end{enumerate}
Changing thread counts cannot repair weak identification, and increasing the
iteration limit cannot repair an incorrect parallel likelihood. Diagnose in
the order used below.

\section{Decision tree}

\begin{center}
\resizebox{0.98\linewidth}{!}{%
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  node distance=7mm and 12mm,
  every node/.style={font=\small,align=center},
  action/.style={rectangle,rounded corners,draw=blue!55!black,fill=blue!4,
    text width=4.1cm,minimum height=9mm},
  decision/.style={diamond,aspect=2.2,draw=black!70,fill=gray!7,
    text width=3.1cm,inner sep=1.5pt},
  arrow/.style={-{Latex[length=2mm]},thick}
]
\node[action] (start) {Record version, model arguments, starts, and timings};
\node[decision,below=of start] (valid) {Parallel checks safe?};
\node[action,left=of valid,xshift=-22mm] (serial) {Use the safe serial path; investigate backend consistency before tuning};
\node[decision,below=of valid] (conv) {Raw-gradient criterion met?};
\node[action,right=of conv,xshift=22mm] (done) {Proceed to uncertainty and transformed summaries};
\node[decision,below=of conv] (progress) {Objective and gradient still improve materially?};
\node[action,left=of progress,xshift=-22mm] (refine) {Continue once from saved estimates with a smaller step};
\node[decision,below=of progress] (stable) {Substantive transformed estimates stable?};
\node[action,right=of stable,xshift=22mm] (report) {Document practical stability and remaining raw-scale warning};
\node[action,left=of stable,xshift=-22mm] (identify) {Inspect study information, local parameters, Hessian, and model restrictions};
\draw[arrow] (start)--(valid);
\draw[arrow] (valid)--node[above]{no}(serial);
\draw[arrow] (valid)--node[right]{yes}(conv);
\draw[arrow] (conv)--node[above]{yes}(done);
\draw[arrow] (conv)--node[right]{no}(progress);
\draw[arrow] (progress)--node[above]{yes}(refine);
\draw[arrow] (progress)--node[right]{no}(stable);
\draw[arrow] (stable)--node[above]{yes}(report);
\draw[arrow] (stable)--node[above]{no}(identify);
\end{tikzpicture}
}
\end{center}

\section{Step 1: preserve a reproducible diagnostic record}

Record the installed version and preserve every expensive fit before further
experimentation.

<<record-fit, purl=FALSE>>=
packageVersion("CoTiMA2")
saveRDS(fit_julia, "fit_julia.rds")

fit_julia$optimization
fit_julia$optimization_timing
fit_julia$likelihood_timing
fit_julia$threads
fit_julia$optimizer_controls
fit_julia$warm_start
@

The most useful convergence fields are the termination code, the maximum
absolute raw gradient, the objective, the number of iterations, and the change
from the starting point. A termination such as \code{Iterations} means that
the iteration limit was reached; it does not by itself establish that the
solution is poor. Conversely, a stable objective does not establish that all
parameters are identified.

\section{Step 2: verify the parallel backend before benchmarking}

Use \code{ctmaFitConfig()} with exactly the same model-defining arguments that
will be supplied to \code{ctmaFit()}. Binary-variable and random-intercept
arguments belong at the top level, whereas computational controls belong in
\code{backendArgs}.

<<fit-config, purl=FALSE>>=
fit_config <- ctmaFitConfig(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  backend = "julia",
  binaries = c(1, 0),
  randomIntercepts = "CINT",
  scaleTime = 1/12
)

fit_config$parallel_backend_check
fit_config$benchmarks
fit_config$optimizer_pilots
fit_config$recommended
@

Interpret the consistency check before interpreting speed:
\begin{itemize}
  \item If likelihood and gradient are both safe, parallel subject processing
    may be used for optimization.
  \item If either check fails, CoTiMA2 retains the deterministic safe path.
    Increasing \code{subject\_threads} then cannot accelerate the affected
    calculation.
  \item The choice is made for the fit and retained. It is not repeatedly
    switched during optimization.
\end{itemize}

\important{A recommended thread configuration is a performance recommendation,
not a guarantee of convergence. The benchmark measures a small number of
likelihood and gradient evaluations; the full optimizer may visit much more
difficult parameter regions.}

\section{Step 3: inspect optimizer progress}

Use the complete optimization record rather than only the last console line.

<<optimizer-record, purl=FALSE>>=
list(
  objective = -fit_julia$optimization$loglik,
  iterations = fit_julia$optimization$iterations,
  stage_iterations = fit_julia$optimization$stage_iterations,
  stage_objectives = fit_julia$optimization$stage_objectives,
  stage_gradients =
    fit_julia$optimization$stage_maximum_absolute_gradients,
  maximum_raw_gradient =
    fit_julia$optimization$maximum_absolute_gradient,
  termination = fit_julia$optimization$termination_code,
  converged = fit_julia$optimization$converged
)
@

With \code{carefulFit=TRUE}, the three stages are:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item population parameters while TI effects are held fixed;
  \item population and TI-effect parameters together;
  \item a jointly rescaled refinement.
\end{enumerate}
When valid estimates are explicitly supplied through \code{inits}, CoTiMA2 can
avoid unnecessarily rebuilding early starting values. Always ensure that the
new model has exactly the same raw parameterization and parameter ordering.

\section{Step 4: compare a refinement with its starting fit}

A refinement should be judged by objective improvement, gradient reduction,
and parameter stability together.

<<compare-fits, purl=FALSE>>=
old_raw <- fit_previous$coefficients$raw
new_raw <- fit_refined$coefficients$raw
raw_names <- names(coef(fit_refined, type = "raw"))
if (is.null(raw_names)) raw_names <- paste0("raw", seq_along(new_raw))

final_gradient <- as.numeric(fit_refined$optimization$gradient)
parameter_change <- new_raw - old_raw

gradient_diagnostics <- data.frame(
  index = seq_along(new_raw),
  parameter = raw_names,
  estimate = new_raw,
  change = parameter_change,
  gradient = final_gradient,
  absolute_gradient = abs(final_gradient),
  absolute_change = abs(parameter_change)
)

head(
  gradient_diagnostics[
    order(gradient_diagnostics$absolute_gradient, decreasing = TRUE),
  ],
  25
)

head(
  gradient_diagnostics[
    order(gradient_diagnostics$absolute_change, decreasing = TRUE),
  ],
  25
)
@

Large gradients with small parameter changes indicate a stiff direction.
Large parameter changes with tiny gradients indicate a flat direction. Large
raw changes may also cancel between a population parameter and its TI effect,
or may correspond to small changes after nonlinear constraints are applied.

\section{Step 5: diagnose the study design}

\subsection{Rank and conditioning of study indicators}

<<study-design, purl=FALSE>>=
standata <- prepared_diagnostic$fit$standata
tipreds <- as.matrix(standata$tipredsdata)
colnames(tipreds) <- paste0("TI", seq_len(ncol(tipreds)))

weighted_design <- cbind(Intercept = 1, tipreds)
unique_design <- unique(weighted_design)

list(
  subject_level_rank = qr(weighted_design)$rank,
  subject_level_columns = ncol(weighted_design),
  subject_level_condition_number = kappa(weighted_design, exact = TRUE),
  study_level_rank = qr(unique_design)$rank,
  study_level_columns = ncol(unique_design),
  study_level_condition_number = kappa(unique_design, exact = TRUE),
  study_level_singular_values = svd(unique_design, nu = 0, nv = 0)$d
)
@

Full rank establishes algebraic estimability of the indicator design. It does
not guarantee that every study provides enough longitudinal information for
every dynamic, diffusion, initial-condition, or random-intercept parameter.

\subsection{Persons, rows, and measurement occasions}

<<study-information, purl=FALSE>>=
subject_rows <- tabulate(
  as.integer(standata$subject),
  nbins = nrow(tipreds)
)

study_label <- ifelse(
  rowSums(tipreds) == 0,
  "reference",
  paste0("TI", max.col(tipreds, ties.method = "first"))
)

study_information <- do.call(
  rbind,
  lapply(split(subject_rows, study_label), function(x) {
    data.frame(
      n_subjects = length(x),
      n_rows = sum(x),
      minimum_waves = min(x),
      median_waves = median(x),
      mean_waves = mean(x),
      maximum_waves = max(x)
    )
  })
)

study_information
@

The number of persons is not the only relevant quantity. A study with many
persons but only two or three occasions may weakly identify diffusion or
dynamic covariance parameters. Binary outcomes additionally require adequate
numbers in both categories.

\section{Step 6: reconstruct the parameters actually used by each study}

Under dummy coding, a raw study parameter is generally the population or
reference parameter plus the corresponding TI effect. Inspecting TI effects
alone can therefore exaggerate movement when the two components cancel.

<<study-specific-raw, purl=FALSE>>=
nbase <- as.integer(standata$nparams)
neffects <- length(raw_names) - nbase
effect_setup <- as.matrix(standata$TIPREDEFFECTsetup)

study_specific_raw <- function(raw) {
  base <- raw[seq_len(nbase)]
  effects <- raw[nbase + seq_len(neffects)]
  designs <- unique(tipreds)
  result <- matrix(
    rep(base, times = nrow(designs)),
    nrow = nrow(designs), byrow = TRUE
  )
  for (s in seq_len(nrow(designs))) {
    for (p in seq_len(nbase)) {
      for (j in seq_len(ncol(designs))) {
        effect_index <- effect_setup[p, j]
        if (effect_index > 0 && designs[s, j] != 0) {
          result[s, p] <- result[s, p] +
            effects[effect_index] * designs[s, j]
        }
      }
    }
  }
  rownames(result) <- ifelse(
    rowSums(designs) == 0,
    "reference",
    paste0("TI", max.col(designs, ties.method = "first"))
  )
  colnames(result) <- raw_names[seq_len(nbase)]
  result
}

local_previous <- study_specific_raw(old_raw)
local_current <- study_specific_raw(new_raw)
local_change <- local_current - local_previous

local_change_diagnostics <- data.frame(
  study = rep(rownames(local_change), ncol(local_change)),
  parameter = rep(colnames(local_change), each = nrow(local_change)),
  previous = as.vector(local_previous),
  current = as.vector(local_current),
  change = as.vector(local_change)
)
local_change_diagnostics$absolute_change <-
  abs(local_change_diagnostics$change)

head(
  local_change_diagnostics[
    order(local_change_diagnostics$absolute_change, decreasing = TRUE),
  ],
  30
)
@

\section{Step 7: compare constrained point estimates without a Hessian}

Raw ctsem parameters may be unconstrained optimization coordinates. Apply
ctsem's transformations without re-optimizing or calculating uncertainty by
using \code{ctmaTransformJuliaFit()}.

<<point-transform, purl=FALSE>>=
previous_transformed <- ctmaTransformJuliaFit(
  fit_previous,
  setup = prepared_diagnostic
)

refined_transformed <- ctmaTransformJuliaFit(
  fit_refined,
  setup = prepared_diagnostic
)

previous_transformed$coefficients$matrices
refined_transformed$coefficients$matrices

previous_transformed$coefficients$study
refined_transformed$coefficients$study
@

\warningbox{This function returns point transformations only. It does not
create standard errors, confidence intervals, a covariance matrix, or evidence
of convergence. Use \code{ctAddJuliaSamples()} only after reliable uncertainty
information is available.}

If the constrained matrices and scientifically relevant effects are stable
while a few raw coordinates continue to move, the remaining problem is likely
to be raw-scale curvature or weak identification. If the constrained effects
also change materially, the fit should not yet be interpreted.

\section{Step 8: use the Hessian only after point diagnostics}

The observed Hessian is useful for locating flat and boundary directions, but
it can be expensive and should not be calculated merely because optimization
stopped. Inspect its symmetric eigenvalues and condition number when the point
diagnostics remain ambiguous.

<<hessian-diagnostics, purl=FALSE>>=
hessian_symmetric <- (raw_hessian + t(raw_hessian)) / 2
eigenvalues <- eigen(hessian_symmetric, symmetric = TRUE)$values
positive <- eigenvalues[eigenvalues > 0]

list(
  minimum = min(eigenvalues),
  maximum = max(eigenvalues),
  nonpositive = sum(eigenvalues <= 0),
  near_zero = sum(abs(eigenvalues) < 1e-6),
  positive_condition_number = max(positive) / min(positive),
  smallest = head(sort(eigenvalues), 10)
)
@

A few very small negative eigenvalues can reflect numerical error near a flat
optimum. Substantial negative curvature, many near-zero eigenvalues, or an
extreme condition number require closer model inspection. Regularizing a
Hessian can make inversion possible, but does not create information that the
data do not contain.

\section{Reference studies, dummy coding, and WEC}

Changing the reference study does not change the likelihood surface in exact
arithmetic when the models are algebraically equivalent. It does change the
raw coordinates, starting values, scaling, and optimizer path. Consequently,
a different reference study can genuinely make numerical optimization easier
even though it cannot solve structural non-identification.

A useful reference study is usually reasonably large, has many informative
measurement occasions, contains both categories of every binary outcome, and
is not close to a variance or correlation boundary. A very small or atypical
reference study places the common raw intercept at a poorly determined point
and forces many large study deviations.

Weighted effect coding can reduce dependence on one arbitrary reference and
place the population parameter closer to a weighted average study. This may
improve scaling. It does not add information and cannot remove a likelihood
ridge. CoTiMA now separates the contrast basis from the parameter families:

\begin{verbatim}
studyCoding = "weighted"
studyVarying = c("CINT", "DIFFUSION", "T0VAR")
\end{verbatim}

Add \code{"DRIFT"} only when study-specific drift parameters are intended.
The older \code{WEC=TRUE} interface is deprecated and maps to the weighted,
parameter-selective specification above. This coding is constructed by
\code{ctmaFit()} and is therefore identical for Stan and Julia backends.

\section{Reproducible validation of the native ctsem Julia backend}

\subsection{Bayesian NUTS and SAEM diagnostics}

From CoTiMA2 0.14.29, \code{ctmaInit()} and \code{ctmaFit()} accept native
Bayesian sampling with \code{backend="ctsemjulia"},
\code{optimize=FALSE}, and \code{priors=TRUE}. Since 0.14.33, omit sampler
and placement for native automatic selection, or select \code{"nuts"},
\code{"saem"}, \code{"map"} or \code{"smc"} through
\code{backendArgs\$sampleControl\$sampler}. Set the integration target
separately through \code{backendArgs\$intoverpop}. The mode records the
resolved target (\code{"joint"}, \code{"laplace"}, \code{"augmented"},
or \code{"direct"}). Inspect divergences and other kernel diagnostics where
the native sampler reports them; SAEM can itself use NUTS updates, so its
name does not imply the absence of Hamiltonian sampling diagnostics.

For either sampler, inspect chain agreement, effective sample sizes, trace
plots, and Monte Carlo error for the substantive parameters and random-effect
standard deviations. Use the native \code{summary(fit)} and available plot
methods. Record \code{chains}, \code{warmup}, \code{draws},
\code{maxDraws}, the seed, and the actual number of retained draws.
\code{maxDraws} allows extension towards diagnostic targets, so completing
the requested initial draw count need not end sampling. Reaching the maximum
budget also does not establish convergence.

For NUTS, additionally inspect divergences and maximum-tree-depth events.
The ML validation suite below checks optimizer convergence and Laplace
accuracy; it does not establish posterior convergence for a Bayesian run.
For SAEM joint sampling, a Laplace correction check is not the primary
sampling diagnostic. Compare runtime using effective samples per second,
and state explicitly when the compared fits use different integration targets.

The package source contains a separate engineering validation suite for the
experimental \code{backend="ctsemjulia"}.  It simulates known subject- and
study-level heterogeneity. Random-effect distributions are generated on
ctsem's unconstrained raw scale, whereas \code{popsd.study} reports the induced
standard deviation after the parameter transformation. The suite therefore
stores both targets and compares the reported estimate with the corresponding
reported-scale truth. For diagonal T0VAR, the transformed value is the
initial-state standard-deviation factor, not the variance itself. The full suite
tests CINT, DRIFT, DIFFUSION, and T0VAR and follows each fit with adaptive
quadrature through \code{ctLaplaceCheck()}.

The same backend can also fit models without random effects.  In that case,
\code{fit$ctsemjulia_mode} is \code{"direct"} and Laplace diagnostics are
neither required nor meaningful.  A controlled Ryzen smoke test with 800
persons and 9,600 rows gave the same log likelihood (rounded to $-13000.3$)
for CoTiMA2's Julia engine and native ctsem Julia; both converged.  With
post-fit Hessian work disabled in both paths, the end-to-end times were about
32.6 and 17.9 seconds, respectively.  This small test includes different
startup and safety-check overhead and must not be generalized as a universal
speed ratio.  Benchmark the intended model shape and inspect both estimates
and convergence, not elapsed time alone.

Current ctsem development versions store optimizer diagnostics in
\code{fit\$optim} and parameter estimates and uncertainty in
\code{fit\$estimate}. CoTiMA2 preserves these native components and adds a
stable compact view in \code{fit\$ctsemjulia\_diagnostics}. In particular,
\code{fit\$laplace\$inner\_converged} is a single overall flag; it is not the
number of groups. The final and expected counts are available as
\code{fit\$ctsemjulia\_diagnostics\$inner\_modes\$converged} and
\code{expected}.

For study-level Laplace models, the native \code{subject\_loglik} vector is
not necessarily a personwise decomposition of the marginal likelihood. It
must not be supplied to LOO or WAIC when
\code{fit\$ctsemjulia\_diagnostics\$casewise\_loglik\$usable\_for\_loo} is
false. CoTiMA2 retains the original vector but prints a note and records the
reason in the diagnostic component.

After a successful fit, save an immutable reference result before conducting
sensitivity analyses:

<<native-reference-fit, purl=FALSE>>=
saveRDS(fit_random_drift, "fit_random_drift_reference.rds")

fit_random_drift$ctsemjulia_diagnostics
fit_random_drift$identifiability
fit_random_drift$intervalcheck
@

An inner-mode tolerance sensitivity fit should reuse the converged raw
estimates, change only the inner solver controls, and then compare the
substantive estimates and marginal log likelihood. For example:

<<native-inner-sensitivity, purl=FALSE>>=
sensitivity_args <- CoTiMA2::ctmaBackendDefaults("ctsemjulia")
sensitivity_args$study_random_effects <- "DRIFT"
sensitivity_args$inits <- fit_random_drift$estimate$raw
sensitivity_args$optimcontrol <- list(
  laplace_inner_maxiter = 500L,
  laplace_inner_tol = 1e-5,
  uncertainty = "hessian"
)

fit_random_drift_sensitivity <- CoTiMA2::ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  backend = "ctsemjulia",
  coresToUse = 4L,
  backendArgs = sensitivity_args
)

c(
  reference_loglik = fit_random_drift$estimate$loglik,
  sensitivity_loglik = fit_random_drift_sensitivity$estimate$loglik,
  maximum_raw_change = max(abs(
    fit_random_drift$estimate$raw -
      fit_random_drift_sensitivity$estimate$raw
  ))
)
@

The sensitivity fit supports the reference result when both fits converge,
all inner modes converge, and the log likelihood and transformed substantive
estimates remain practically unchanged. A stricter inner tolerance that fails
to converge is not evidence against the reference fit; it indicates that the
inner solver needs more iterations or that the requested tolerance is too
strict for the current parameterization.

The single-run suite defaults to fixed standardized normal quantiles for the
study effects. This deterministic design is useful as a regression test. The
Monte Carlo controller instead defaults to independently sampled study
effects in every replication (\code{--study-effects=random}), which is
required when interpreting bias, RMSE, and interval coverage across simulated
study populations. Either behavior can be selected explicitly with
\code{--study-effects=quantiles} or \code{--study-effects=random}.

Open Terminal, change to the CoTiMA2 project directory, and execute:

\begin{verbatim}
Rscript tools/ctsemjulia-validation-suite.R --mode=quick --cores=4
\end{verbatim}

The more informative full run is started with:

\begin{verbatim}
Rscript tools/ctsemjulia-validation-suite.R --mode=full --cores=4
\end{verbatim}

The same commands work under macOS and Ubuntu.  If a separate R library
contains the required ctsem development version, add
\code{--development-library=/path/to/library}.  If Julia is not discovered,
add \code{--julia-bindir=/path/to/julia/bin}.  Complete instructions are in
\code{tools/CTSEMJULIA\_VALIDATION.md}.

The run creates \code{REPORT.md}, \code{summary.csv}, and
\code{validation-results.rds} under
\code{~/CoTiMA\_Benchmark/ctsemjulia-validation/}.  A PASS is an engineering
screen, not proof of unbiasedness or correct interval coverage.  Replication
over multiple seeds and model dimensions remains necessary.

For a resumable Monte Carlo validation, use the separate controller.  Each
replication runs in an independent R/Julia process, and completed scenario
files are reused after an interruption only when their scenario, seed, and
design dimensions match:

\begin{verbatim}
Rscript tools/ctsemjulia-monte-carlo.R \
  --mode=full --replications=10 \
  --workers=4 --cores-per-fit=4
\end{verbatim}

The aggregate report includes convergence rates, bias, relative bias, RMSE,
absolute error, Laplace/quadrature gaps, execution times, and 95\% interval
coverage when ctsem exposes finite interval limits.  Use one worker on a
laptop initially.  On a many-core workstation, first benchmark a small batch
and ensure that \code{workers * cores-per-fit} leaves at least one physical
core free.  Full details, output files, and restart options are described in
\code{tools/CTSEMJULIA\_VALIDATION.md}.

The first completed reference experiment used 50 independently simulated data
sets for each of CINT, DRIFT, DIFFUSION, and T0VAR.  All 200 fits converged;
the transformed-scale targets, bias, RMSE, coverage, and limitations are
recorded in
\code{tools/CTSEMJULIA\_MONTE\_CARLO\_RESULTS\_0.14.15.md}.

After these single-family checks, the script
\code{tools/ctsemjulia-joint-validation.R} estimates study-level variation in
CINT, DRIFT, DIFFUSION, and T0VAR simultaneously, together with a person-level
CINT random effect.  It simulates mutually independent study effects.  The
current native ctsem Laplace implementation nevertheless estimates a full
raw-scale correlation matrix, so the joint test also checks whether the six
freely estimated correlations remain close to their true value of zero.  A
quick run should precede the full design; \code{--simulate-only=true} checks
data generation without fitting.

The completed quick and full joint experiments are summarized in
\code{tools/CTSEMJULIA\_JOINT\_RESULTS\_0.14.15.md}.  Both optimizations
converged with gradient norms below $6\times10^{-9}$, and the full design
recovered all four study SDs within approximately 11 percent.  Nevertheless,
the adaptive-quadrature comparison gave Laplace gaps of 0.0398 and 0.0316 per
person, respectively.  Thus increasing the number of studies stabilized the
SD and correlation estimates but did not eliminate the approximation warning.

The option \code{--components=} selects a nested subset of the four study
random-effect families, while \code{--check-nodes=} controls the diagnostic
quadrature.  The resumable script
\code{tools/ctsemjulia-dimensional-validation.R} compares all six pairwise
combinations, CINT+DRIFT+DIFFUSION, and all four families in fresh R/Julia
processes.  The complete pairwise design identifies parameter-specific
interactions, while the three- and four-dimensional fits reveal a more general
dimensionality effect.

Do not treat a non-hierarchical Stan or CoTiMA2-Julia fit as a numerical gold
standard for this validation.  Such a model estimates study fixed effects or
a pooled fixed effect, whereas the native Laplace model integrates Gaussian
random effects.  Their likelihoods and estimands are therefore different.  A
speed comparison is meaningful only after the competing backends express the
same hierarchical model.

\subsection{A standardized covariance-stationary validation}

The preceding engineering experiments deliberately treat DRIFT, DIFFUSION,
and T0VAR as separate random-effect families.  A complementary validation is
needed for the stronger, idealized assumption that the \emph{latent} process
covariance is standardized and stationary.  The script
\code{tools/ctsemjulia-standardized-validation.R} fixes marginal latent
variances at one and derives process noise from
\[
  \bm Q_s=-(\bm A_s\bm P_s+\bm P_s\bm A_s^\top).
\]
DIFFUSION consequently has no independent study random effect.  The quick and
full modes test a univariate study-varying drift, a bivariate model with two
study-varying diagonal drift parameters and a fixed zero T0 correlation, and a
bivariate model in which a third raw study effect defines a bounded T0
correlation while both marginal T0 variances remain exactly one.

This restriction is stronger than merely standardizing the observed variables
at every occasion.  Measurement error, finite-sample standardization, and
nonstationarity can all make the latent covariance differ from the observed
unit-variance covariance.  The validation must therefore be interpreted as a
test of a particular stationary latent data-generating model, not as an
automatic property of every CoTiMA correlation input.

The native Julia backend previously rejected parameter-dependent expressions
in T0VAR.  The \code{juliaFit} development branch now resolves composed static
T0VAR expressions over multiple PARS coordinates and differentiates them.  A
smoke test and the standardized validation therefore use
\code{rho = 0.3 * tanh(rho\_raw)} in the off-diagonal T0VAR cell, keep both
diagonal entries fixed at one, and derive the complete DIFFUSION factor from
DRIFT and this target covariance.  This support applies to static initial-state
expressions.  Time-dependent predictors and latent-state references do not
define one initial covariance and remain outside the supported scope.

In the full design, all six start configurations converged to the same
solution.  Two independent simulation seeds recovered the generating raw
study SDs $(0.20,0.16)$ as $(0.159,0.169)$ and $(0.214,0.185)$; their
95-percent intervals covered both generating values.  The corresponding
Laplace--quadrature gaps were $2.78\times10^{-4}$ and
$3.47\times10^{-4}$ per person.  Direct numerical checks reproduced the
Lyapunov covariance to within $4.44\times10^{-16}$.

The extended full validation also converged when the T0 correlation varied by
study.  It estimated the generating raw study SDs $(0.20,0.16,0.18)$ as
$(0.196,0.150,0.244)$ and showed a Laplace--quadrature discrepancy of
$1.81\times10^{-4}$ per person.  A reporting bug initially displayed the third
SD as \code{NA}: the validator had reduced the truth label
\code{rho\_raw\_sd} to \code{rho} instead of matching \code{rho\_raw}.  The
corrected full fit had no unidentified Hessian direction
(\code{nweak=0}), no negative direction, and an identifiability condition
number of 30,699.  A separate eight-study diagnostic did contain flat
correlation directions, but it had only eight outer-level units for nine free
population parameters and was therefore rank-limited by construction.  It
must not be used to diagnose the 16-study fit.

Six independent repetitions of the 16-study correlation design all
converged, with a maximum gradient norm of $8.35\times10^{-9}$ and a maximum
Laplace--quadrature gap of $2.12\times10^{-4}$ per person.  Across repetitions,
the generating study SDs $(0.20,0.16,0.18)$ had mean estimates
$(0.188,0.189,0.183)$, median estimates $(0.182,0.200,0.187)$, and RMSEs
$(0.052,0.066,0.026)$.  Thus, recovery was best for the raw T0-correlation SD
and most variable for the second drift SD.  Three of the six fits flagged one
weak random-effect-correlation direction: twice the correlation between the
two drift effects and once the correlation between the T0-correlation effect
and the first drift effect.  These warnings varied by simulated data set and
did not prevent convergence, but they show that a formally adequate number of
studies does not guarantee stable estimation of every element of a fully
unstructured random-effect covariance matrix.

The current native multilevel API constructs a full covariance among all
study-varying parameters.  CoTiMA2's \code{covariance="independent"},
\code{"equicorrelated"}, and block specifications belong to the separate
\code{ctmaRePrep()} workflow and are not yet transmitted to
\code{backend="ctsemjulia"}.  A structured-covariance API would permit more
parsimonious models, but the full 16-study covariance was identified in this
validation.  Users should nevertheless inspect
\code{fit$identifiability} and the random-effect-correlation section of
\code{summary(fit)}.  A marginal study SD can be estimable even when one or
more correlations involving that effect are not.

For focused repetitions, \code{--scenarios=} accepts a comma-separated subset
of \code{univariate\_drift}, \code{bivariate\_drift}, and
\code{bivariate\_drift\_correlation}.  For example, a new seed can repeat only
the correlation scenario:

\begin{verbatim}
Rscript tools/ctsemjulia-standardized-validation.R \
  --mode=full \
  --scenarios=bivariate_drift_correlation \
  --seed=20260920 \
  --cores=4
\end{verbatim}

Independent seed directories can subsequently be combined with
\code{tools/summarize-ctsemjulia-standardized-multiseed.R}.  Its report keeps
scenarios separate and summarizes mean and median recovery, empirical spread,
RMSE, convergence, gradient norms, identifiability, and Laplace gaps.

\section{A concise stopping rule}

Do not continue merely because the raw gradient exceeds a conventional target.
Continue a refinement when the objective, gradient, and constrained estimates
are still improving materially. Stop and investigate identification when:
\begin{itemize}
  \item additional hours produce negligible objective improvement;
  \item large raw movements occur in directions with very small gradients;
  \item population and TI effects cancel in the study-specific parameters;
  \item constrained estimates are stable but raw coordinates drift; or
  \item the Hessian has near-zero directions or extreme conditioning.
\end{itemize}

When reporting a practically stable but formally non-converged solution,
retain the warning, document the tolerance and termination code, report the
refinement comparison, and avoid presenting Hessian-based uncertainty as
routine inferential output.

\section{Observed native validation outcomes, 27 October 2026}

Eighteen small real-fit tests of CoTiMA2 0.14.33 with ctsem revision
\code{57a9758d} completed on Ubuntu/Ryzen: five ML, five MAP and eight Bayesian
runs. Every optimizer reported convergence, but the study-only ML case warned
that \code{popsd\_c.study} was unidentified, and the mixed binary/continuous
ML case warned that a binary-process drift was unidentified. This demonstrates
why convergence, identification and posterior diagnostics must be separate
decisions. The warnings were preserved; no artificial SE was substituted.

The Bayesian smoke tests reported no divergences. Their short two-chain runs
used relaxed diagnostic targets; finite worst R-hat values around
1.023--1.065 are not sufficient for a final analysis. Inspect all substantive
parameters with longer sampling and appropriate diagnostics. SMC uses particle
diagnostics, so an absent conventional R-hat is not evidence of a failure.

Study-level fits also retained the adapter's warning that a personwise
\code{subject\_loglik} decomposition may not be valid for shared study
effects. Do not use such values for personwise LOO or WAIC; inspect
\code{ctsemjulia\_diagnostics\$casewise\_loglik}. These tests validate the
real native dispatcher, including summary generation and joint/marginal route
selection; they are not a parameter-recovery study or an end-to-end test of
every public model-construction option.

\section{Drift-scaled coordinates: a limited comparison}

An additional Ryzen test of ctsem revision \code{57a9758d} compared standard
coordinates, drift-scaled DIFFUSION, and drift-scaled DIFFUSION plus CINT.
Eighteen ML/MAP fits used 96 people with six dense, coarse or irregular
observations, explicitly disabling individual random effects. Matched initial
likelihoods agreed within $1.3\times10^{-6}$.
Dense and irregular ML fits reached the same likelihood in ten reported
iterations with every parameterization. Coarse ML fits retained identification
warnings despite reported convergence. No consistent numerical benefit was
demonstrated, and subsecond timings do not establish a general speedup.

\code{driftScaled} is a reparameterization, not a constraint imposing
stationarity or unit variance. For MAP or Bayesian estimation, changing the
coordinates without consistently transforming the priors changes the physical
prior. These tests do not justify automatically enabling this option and do
not validate binary or random-effects models.

\section{Binary-process identification: a small paired pilot}

A Ryzen pilot with ctsem revision \code{57a9758d} compared 48 ML fits:
100 or 400 people, eight irregular waves, balanced or approximately 10\%
positive binary responses, three datasets per condition, free or fixed
diffusion, and CINT or MANIFESTMEANS. There were no random or cross-lag
effects. The continuous companion process did not anchor the binary latent
scale. Fitted initial means and variances were free; stationarity was not
imposed. Fixed diffusion used its known generating value, not a value inferred
from the binary observations.

All optimizers reported convergence, but ten runs warned about identification
or saturated transforms. Free diffusion could approach zero or become
extremely large. Fixing it at the truth helped in some conditions, but did not
guarantee drift recovery. Only three replications were used per condition;
these results do not establish bias, interval coverage or a universal remedy.

Two of 24 CINT/MANIFESTMEANS pairs reached different local solutions. Mapping
the estimates between equivalent models reproduced their likelihoods within
$8\times10^{-11}$; both solution families existed in both parameterizations.
Switching intercepts therefore did not remove the problem. Check likelihood
profiles, multiple starting solutions, identification warnings and extreme
parameter values. Numerical accuracy of extreme-variance solutions also
requires independent checking. These results do not prove structural
nonidentification of diffusion in every binary model.

Do not automatically fix diffusion or interpret a fixed parameter's zero
conditional SE as high precision. A fixed value requires an explicit modelling
or scale assumption and sensitivity analysis. Native identification warnings
take precedence over finite Hessian-derived SEs.

\subsection{Independent verification of extreme binary solutions}

A follow-up to the preceding pilot independently integrated the scalar binary
latent process on grids up to 6401 cells, retaining its non-Gaussian filtering
density, and checked the results by Monte Carlo integration of complete latent
trajectories. For two extreme diffusion solutions, native ctsem revision
\code{57a9758d} reported log likelihoods about 55.83 and 16.35 points higher
than the reference. Final grid refinements changed these values by less than
0.00007. The two methods agreed within Monte Carlo uncertainty, and the
reference reversed the native ranking of the paired solutions in both cases.
Fresh ctsem models reproduced the native values without the CoTiMA2 adapter.

Numerical likelihood approximation is therefore materially involved in these
particular extreme solutions; the pilot cannot be interpreted solely as weak
statistical identification. The separate effects of scalar quadrature and
Gaussian posterior projection have not been isolated. Stable control cases
had much smaller discrepancies. Do not interpret these extreme estimates or
their Hessian SEs as validated results merely because the optimizer converged.
This finding concerns the pinned native ctsem revision, not all Julia engines
or future versions. No implementation or default was changed, and the
reference was evaluated at fitted points rather than independently optimized.

\section{Separate fit checks in CoTiMA2 0.14.34}

New native Julia fits retain their ctsem classes and add a CoTiMA2 summary
footer separating optimizer convergence, stored identification problems,
transform saturation and posterior diagnostics. For Bayesian fits the optimizer
line refers only to placement, not MCMC convergence. Primary-study reports
retain study numbers. Missing fields are reported as unknown rather than
interpreted as successful checks.

<<separate-fit-checks, eval=FALSE, purl=FALSE>>=
checks <- ctmaFitDiagnostics(fit, print = FALSE)
ctmaFitDiagnostics(fit)  # also works with older stored fits
summary(fit)            # automatic footer for new native fits
@

New native fits cache population matrices while Julia is already running.
Later diagnostic reports do not start Julia. A binary linear-predictor SD above
10 at Time 0 or at the population stationary covariance is labelled a heuristic
scale flag. It is invariant to reciprocal changes of latent units and loadings;
it is not an identification test, a validated error threshold or a reason to
automatically fix diffusion. Older fits without these matrices report the scale
check as unavailable. Convergence does not certify identification, reliable SEs
or likelihood accuracy. No estimates or standard errors are altered.


\section{ctsem 4 compatibility and stored sampling diagnostics}

CoTiMA2 0.14.42 implements ctsem 4's \code{covmattransform = "z"}
in its bundled \code{backend = "julia"} covariance converter. This is a
normalised matrix exponential of symmetric off-diagonal coordinates, scaled by
marginal standard deviations; these cells must not be read as a Cholesky factor.
Older bundled-engine fits made from ctsem 4 setup with nonzero covariance cells
should be re-fitted. The native \code{backend = "ctsemjulia"} uses ctsem's own
converter and was not affected by this CoTiMA2 conversion defect. Diagonal-only
checks cannot detect this difference. Unknown future covariance codes are
rejected before starting the bundled engine.

For native Bayesian fitting, \code{iter} inside the \code{sampleControl}
list in \code{backendArgs} is now accepted and forwarded alongside ctsem's existing \code{warmup}, \code{draws}
and \code{maxDraws} controls. Their meanings and precedence remain ctsem's;
\code{iter} is not silently renamed to retained draws. Automatic sampler
selection remains delegated to ctsem. In particular, \code{sampler = "map"}
means learned-map posterior sampling, not MAP estimation. MAP estimation uses
optimisation with priors.

\code{ctmaFitDiagnostics(fit)} reads stored information without calculating a
new summary or starting Julia. It handles both a native pooled model stored
directly in \code{studyFitList} and ctmaInit's list of primary fits. Placement
optimizer convergence and reported posterior convergence are separate. Raw
coordinate R-hat and the worst available retained transformed-summary R-hat
are labelled separately, with their effective sample size diagnostics retaining
the source definitions. Missing transformed diagnostics are unknown. SMC does
not have the usual between-chain R-hat; this is reported as not applicable,
with its available ESS retained. A clean report does not certify likelihood
accuracy or statistical identification.

CoTiMA2 explicitly retains \code{uncertainty = "hessian"} within
\code{optimcontrol} for native optimized fits. Current ctsem 4 defaults to \code{"checked"}; users may
request that explicitly. This compatibility repair does not change the chosen
uncertainty method automatically. Use \code{ctJuliaInstall()} to select ctsem's
supported Julia runtime rather than hard-coding an old Juliaup version path.


\section{Optimization targets and posterior uncertainty (version 0.14.43)}

The bundled \code{backend="julia"} optimizer maximizes the likelihood.
It does not implement MAP optimization. In \code{ctmaInit()}, \code{ctmaFit()}
and \code{ctmaFitConfig()}, requesting \code{optimize=TRUE, priors=TRUE}
with this backend now raises an error before fitting. Earlier versions could
silently return an ML fit for that request; such results must not be interpreted
as MAP estimates. Use \code{backend="ctsemjulia"} or \code{backend="stan"}
with \code{optimize=TRUE, priors=TRUE} for MAP. This restriction does not remove
bundled Julia Bayesian NUTS sampling with \code{optimize=FALSE}.

Uncertainty from bundled NUTS sampling is based on retained joint posterior
draws. It does not require a positive-definite likelihood Hessian at the initial
parameter vector. ML uncertainty still requires adequate optimization convergence
and valid curvature; a failed convergence or curvature check must be inspected,
not bypassed. Short pilot chains may execute successfully and still have poor
R-hat, low effective sample sizes, or divergences. Completion is not evidence of
posterior convergence.

The ctsem 4 interface also removes two historical inert Stan optimizer defaults
and renames the internal matrix registry. CoTiMA2 filters the removed defaults
only if the installed ctsem rejects them and their historical values were used;
unsupported non-default requests raise an error. Older compatible ctsem versions
retain their controls.


\section{Binary offsets and mixed mean specifications (version 0.14.44)}

The vector \code{manifestMeans=c("mmV1", 0)} freely estimates the first
manifest mean and fixes the second to zero. Scalar \code{0} fixes all manifest
means; \code{"auto"} requests automatic free labels. Explicit vectors retain
both parameter labels and numeric fixed values.

A binary measurement offset and a continuous-time process intercept have
different roles. If data are generated as a Bernoulli response with probability
\code{plogis(eta1 - 0.8)} from a centered latent process, a matching reference
uses a binary manifest offset of -0.8, centered initial latent means and zero
process intercepts. One may estimate the offset with the following specification:
\begin{verbatim}
# Relevant arguments for a two-variable binary/continuous reference model:
binaries = c(1, 0),
manifestMeans = c("mmV1", 0),
cint = 0,
T0means = 0
\end{verbatim}
This is appropriate for that generating model, not a universal recommendation
to fix all process intercepts. A free CINT changes the state mean after elapsed
time; it cannot supply a missing initial mean at time zero. Under a centered
symmetric initial Gaussian latent and a fixed symmetric logistic link with zero
manifest offset, the marginal initial binary probability is 0.5. A skewed
observed binary distribution alone does not imply any particular latent variance.

Switching from free CINTs to manifest offsets changes model constraints and
usually the induced prior distribution; it is not merely a harmless numerical
reparameterization. Check the generated model before fitting. A completed fit,
a plausible point estimate or inclusion of a true value in one simulated interval
does not establish posterior convergence or general parameter recovery.


\section{Optimizer convergence and Laplace accuracy}

For native study-level random-effects fits, optimizer convergence and the
accuracy of Laplace integration are separate questions. The optimizer can
converge to the optimum of its approximated objective while a stored comparison
with a more accurate integration method remains unresolved.

\code{ctmaFitDiagnostics(fit)} and the native summary footer now report the
cached Laplace accuracy status independently. \code{ctmaTables()} includes the
same information in table notes. When available, the report includes the stored
integration gap, maximum standardized parameter shift and whether a correction
was applied. These quantities retain their upstream definitions; the total gap
is not silently divided by the number of persons or studies.

A status of \code{unresolved} is neither an accuracy pass nor proof of failure.
A status of \code{checked} without an accompanying verdict says only that an
operation was performed. Missing verdicts remain unassessed. A curvature-based
certification of optimizer convergence does not resolve these integration
questions. Diagnostic reports and table generation read stored metadata only:
they do not rerun \code{ctLaplaceCheck()}, start Julia for this metadata, change
estimates or automatically increase computational budgets.

<<laplace-status-separate, eval=FALSE, purl=FALSE>>=
checks <- ctmaFitDiagnostics(fit, print = FALSE)
checks$optimizer_converged
checks$laplace_accuracy
ctmaTables(fit, parameters = "drift", statistics = c("mean", "2.5", "97.5"),
           format = "none", interactive = FALSE)$notes
@


\section{Reproducible initialization and batch-safe file handling}

Automatic initialization for the Stan study-level random-effects workflow
\code{ctmaReFit()} follows the fitting \code{seed}, including an explicit
override of \code{seed} in the fitting-control list. Chain-specific initialization streams differ,
but repeated calls with the same seed and chain are reproducible. Each
initialization callback restores its caller's random-number state, including
when initialization fails. This concerns initialization; it does not promise
that an entire stochastic fitting call leaves the RNG state unchanged.

\code{ctmaOptimizeInit()} validates its required inputs before running retries.
On Unix systems parallel retries use workers owned by that call; on Windows
retries are sequential. The function does not replace a user's registered
\code{foreach} backend. In \code{ctmaGenData()}, cluster cleanup no longer
replaces restoration of the \code{scipen} option on exit.

When an output file already exists, noninteractive saving now stops with a
clear message unless \code{silentOverwrite = TRUE} was explicitly requested.
Use a new filename or directory to keep both results. Interactive saving still
offers overwrite, skip, rename and cancel; a renamed target is checked again
before an existing file can be replaced. Blank input cancels rather than
waiting indefinitely. These changes do not authorize overwriting user files
by default.

<<safe-study-re-and-saving, eval=FALSE, purl=FALSE>>=
# Reproducible automatic initialization for the Stan study-RE workflow:
fit <- ctmaReFit(prepared, estimation = "bayes", seed = 1234,
                 cores = 2, chains = 2)
# For scripted ctmaInit calls, explicitly choose whether saved files may change:
init <- ctmaInit(primaryStudies = studyList, n.latent = 2,
                 activeDirectory = outputDirectory, silentOverwrite = TRUE)
@


\subsection*{Native quadrature refinement and stored diagnostics}
With a compatible recent experimental ctsem, request
\code{backendArgs = list(intoverpop = "quadrature")} to refine an optimized
Laplace fit with random effects. This option requires \code{optimize = TRUE};
it is not a sampler and is not needed for a model without random effects.
The returned mode records \code{"quadrature"}. Earlier ctsem versions may
not support this explicit route.

\code{ctmaFitDiagnostics(fit)} reports stored likelihood definitions separately:
\code{loglik} with its source method and reported \code{loglik_se}, and
\code{loglik_laplace}. A checked likelihood must not be substituted for the
optimized Laplace objective when comparing optimization results. Optimizer
predicted gain, resolution, convergence tolerance and convergence floor are
shown when stored. These are source-reported diagnostics, not a new common
convergence threshold. Optimizer convergence does not establish posterior
convergence or Laplace accuracy; an unresolved integration check remains
unresolved. Reading these diagnostics starts neither Julia nor a new fit.


\section*{CPU budgets and writable Julia projects (0.14.48)}
\code{coresToUse} is resolved against CPU availability reported by parallelly,
including affinity and scheduler/check restrictions. Negative values retain
available cores minus the requested reserve, with a minimum of one. Automatic
bundled Julia allocation uses this resolved budget; explicit Julia worker,
thread and BLAS combinations that exceed it fail before initialization.
The maximum of the main-process and parallel-worker phases is budgeted, because
these fitting phases run sequentially. Transformation workers are bounded too.
The native \code{ctsemjulia} backend receives the resolved \code{cores} budget;
its internal sampler allocation remains ctsem's responsibility.

Owned child processes inherit single-thread BLAS/OpenMP environment limits;
the caller's environment is restored even after errors. This does not change
the threading of an R BLAS library already loaded by the caller. External
processes and computations in other R sessions are outside this budget.

The bundled \code{julia} backend uses a content-keyed project copy under R's
CoTiMA2 user cache. Installed package files are read-only templates. Project
instantiation may update the cached manifest, while fitting does not install
missing dependencies. An explicit custom Julia source/project remains the
user's responsibility. This policy does not change ctsem's independently
managed native engine cache.

Inspect \code{ctmaJuliaSetup(dry_run = TRUE)} without creating project files
or running Julia. For setup, explicitly run
\code{ctmaJuliaSetup(agree = TRUE)}; batch sessions otherwise stop and
interactive sessions ask for consent. JuliaCall bootstrap can also update
Julia's default user environment. Restart R after bootstrap as instructed.


\section{Systematic optimization and study sensitivity checks}
\label{sec:systematic-optimization}
The historical \code{ctmaOptimizeFit()} search is retained with
\code{gambling = TRUE} (the default). It can try random starts, study orders
and selected settings as before. Set \code{gambling = FALSE} to use a planned
pooled maximum-likelihood workflow instead. This mode retains the saved backend,
observations and time scaling. It currently supports \code{stan} and
\code{ctsemjulia}, dummy study coding and no hierarchical study random effects;
MAP/Bayesian searches and weighted recoding are not supported in this mode.

First check a new reference with all studies retained. The current reference is
the last study in the saved list. A large study is a reasonable numerical first
choice, but is not guaranteed to be the best reference. Changing the reference
must preserve the same model; partially constrained study-effect families that
cannot be safely rebased are rejected. No study is excluded automatically.
\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
# pooledFit is a saved pooled ctmaFit() ML result, with dummy coding.
# Install callr and ps beforehand if they are not available.
reference_check <- ctmaOptimizeFit(
  ctmaFitFit = pooledFit,
  gambling = FALSE,
  referenceStudy = "largestN",
  quickCheck = TRUE,
  quickControl = list(maxiter = 100, maxSeconds = 120),
  coresToUse = 4, timeBudget = 600,
  checkpointDirectory = file.path(activeDirectory, "reference_check")
)
summary(reference_check)
\end{lstlisting}
A pilot is a bounded screening calculation. A finite likelihood, a smaller
gradient or a disappearing warning does not establish identification or validate
standard errors. A timeout (including setup time) is inconclusive. All warnings,
errors and available convergence/curvature diagnostics remain in the returned
object and checkpoint directory. Published ctsem versions that expose no Stan
iteration limit use the subprocess wall-time cap instead, with a recorded
warning. Older Stan APIs may also require their usual uncertainty
postprocessing to return a complete fit with TI predictors. Such pilots retain
only 20 approximate draws and remain inconclusive. The function does not claim a calibrated
probability of successful fitting from these quick checks.

If independent diagnostics and the scientific question justify sensitivity
analyses, nominate studies by their \emph{original study IDs}, not list positions:
\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
# Replace these illustrative IDs with actual originalStudyNo values.
sensitivity <- ctmaOptimizeFit(
  ctmaFitFit = pooledFit, gambling = FALSE,
  candidateStudies = c(9, 10),
  exclusion = c("single", "all"),
  referenceStudy = "largestN",
  quickCheck = TRUE,
  quickControl = list(maxiter = 100, maxSeconds = 120,
                      maxFullChecks = 1, fullMaxSeconds = 600),
  iter = 2000, coresToUse = 4, timeBudget = 1800,
  checkpointDirectory = file.path(activeDirectory, "sensitivity_check")
)
sensitivity$caseDiagnostics
sensitivity$warnings
sensitivity$errors
# Individual pilot/full fits and their plans:
sensitivity$sensitivity$cases
\end{lstlisting}
Use \code{exclusion = "blocks"} with \code{studyBlocks = list(c(9, 10), c(20, 22))}
for nominated blocks. At least two studies must remain. If the selected reference
is removed, the largest retained study becomes the reference. The full-data
current-reference case is always included; duplicate cases are removed.

Full follow-ups occur only when requested through \code{maxFullChecks}, after
all pilots, in planned order. Alternatively, use \code{quickCheck = FALSE} for
bounded fuller checks of every case. The best pilot start is selected only within
the same case. Likelihoods from different study subsets are not comparable for
choosing a winner. \code{bestFit} retains the best full-data attempt, including
the supplied baseline; subset fits never silently replace it. Report exclusions
and effect stability as sensitivity analyses, not evidence that an inconvenient
study should be deleted.

Use \code{resume = TRUE} and the same checkpoint directory and settings to reuse
completed cases. Data, plan or recorded package-version changes invalidate a
resume. Time budgets apply to each call; requesting a resume is an explicit new
budget. A checkpoint retains fit objects and therefore may contain research
data: choose its location accordingly.


\subsection*{Automatic candidate nominations}
Set \code{referenceCandidates = "auto"} to nominate up to three largest
unflagged alternative reference studies. Ties use the median number of distinct
observation times, then the original study order. Set
\code{candidateStudies = "auto"} to nominate up to ten studies from stored
study-local weak-direction diagnostics, or from retained primary fits with
explicit nonconvergence or weak-direction flags. Supply \code{ctmaInitFit} if
these primary fits are in a separate initial fit object; their original IDs
must verify the alignment.

Study-local evidence means a reported weak direction with at most four mapped
study-TI coordinates and no shared or unmapped coefficients. Shared drift
weakness is not assigned to individual studies. Small N or only two occasions
alone do not nominate exclusions. These are descriptive, reference-dependent
flags, not proof that a particular study causes nonidentification. At least two
studies must remain. Inspect all reasons before requesting exclusions:
\noindent\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
auto_check <- ctmaOptimizeFit(
  ctmaFitFit = pooledFit, gambling = FALSE,
  referenceCandidates = "auto",
  candidateStudies = "auto", exclusion = "single",
  planOnly = TRUE, # Review the plan without fitting
  quickCheck = TRUE,
  quickControl = list(maxSeconds = 120),
  coresToUse = 4, timeBudget = 600,
  checkpointDirectory = file.path(activeDirectory, "auto_check")
)
auto_check$automaticSelection$studyDiagnostics
auto_check$automaticSelection$notes
# After scientific review, exclusion="single" requests nominated leave-one-out
# checks; exclusion="all" removes the nominated set together.
\end{lstlisting}
\end{minipage}\par\medskip
\code{planOnly = TRUE} returns \code{casePlan} and the nomination report without starting workers, fitting, likelihood evaluation or checkpoint writes. Set it to \code{FALSE} to run the reviewed plan. Nomination alone never requests exclusion. With no eligible automatic flags,
single/all exclusion cases are skipped and the reason is reported. Block
exclusions still require explicit study IDs. The checkpoint directory contains
\code{automaticStudyDiagnostics.csv}, and \code{maxCases} still limits the plan.


\paragraph{Unassessed curvature.}
The systematic diagnostic table records \code{curvature\_assessed} separately.
When no usable saved Hessian is available, \code{weak\_directions} is
\code{NA}; an engine-initialized zero is not interpreted as a successful
identification check. Legacy zeroes without recorded assessment provenance are
also displayed as unknown. A finite Hessian or zero weak-direction count alone
does not certify optimizer convergence or trustworthy uncertainty.


\section{Stationary initialization and determined diffusion}
The optional arguments \texttt{stationary=FALSE} and
\texttt{determinedDiffusions=FALSE} preserve existing behavior by default.
The experimental determined-diffusion option estimates the stationary
within-person covariance; it does not fix it to the unit covariance of
standardized observed data. It currently supports ML for one or two
unrestricted processes with \texttt{stationary=TRUE}; unsupported process
constraints and study-effect mappings cause explicit errors.
For the supported combinations and model-preparation example, see
\texttt{CoTiMA2\_Stationary\_Processes\_0.14.59.Rnw}.


\subsection{Randomly alternating stationary covariance parametrizations}
For a difficult pooled stationary ML fit, an additional opt-in search can
alternate between estimating diffusion and estimating the stationary within-person
covariance. This does not randomly impose stationarity: the baseline must already
specify \texttt{stationary = TRUE}. The model, studies and scales remain fixed.
The default \texttt{randomDeterminedDiffusions = FALSE} preserves the existing search.
\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
# initFit must be the initialization object used for stationaryFit.
# A common process structure is required for this first implementation.
stationaryFit <- ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = initFit, backend = "ctsemjulia",
  optimize = TRUE, priors = FALSE,
  allInvModel = TRUE, stationary = TRUE
)
search <- ctmaOptimizeFit(
  ctmaFitFit = stationaryFit, ctmaInitFit = initFit,
  gambling = TRUE, randomDeterminedDiffusions = TRUE,
  backend = "ctsemjulia", reFits = 6, seed = 42,
  coresToUse = 2
)
search$attempts
bestFit <- search$bestFit
\end{lstlisting}
Both parametrizations are checked with \texttt{fit = FALSE} before any refit.
Only supported one/two-process Gaussian ML models are accepted; fixed/custom
process constraints and incompatible study effects are rejected. Raw starting
vectors are discarded because the covariance coordinates differ.
This option currently supports pooled \texttt{ctmaFit()} models, not primary-study
or raw ctsem searches, and cannot be combined with the other random model,
scale or study-order switches. \texttt{seed} controls variant choices and R seeds;
the caller's R random-number state is restored. Independent choices may select
only one variant in a short search. \texttt{attempts} records choices, failures,
objective values and available convergence diagnostics. The best finite objective
is not a certification of convergence, identification or valid uncertainty.

\section{Two-wave random intercepts and unavailable uncertainty}
Before fitting each primary study, \texttt{ctmaInit()} checks the number of
observed occasions per person when random intercepts are requested. With at
most two waves, an unrestricted dynamic within-person process and a
random-intercept covariance cannot generally be separated without additional
identifying constraints. A larger sample does not supply additional time
points. Unequal observation intervals and substantive model restrictions can
change identification, so this is a design warning rather than a universal
claim about all two-wave models. Three waves do not guarantee identification.

Optimizer convergence, identification, and interval reliability are separate
checks. A random-intercept variance near zero makes its correlations
uninterpretable, but need not make all drift effects unidentifiable. When
native ctsem explicitly flags a coordinate as having no estimable uncertainty,
CoTiMA2 retains its point estimate and reports the affected standard errors
and intervals as \texttt{NA}, rather than treating a projected numerical
covariance as evidence of precision. Derived covariance uncertainty is also
withheld when its source has no estimable width. Combined summaries retain
study-labelled identification notes. Calling \texttt{summary()} on an older
saved primary-study fit collection refreshes these reporting flags using
stored diagnostics; it does not refit models or start Julia.

Do not automatically exclude a study because it has two waves or a warning.
Inspect the design and fitted diagnostics, and compare substantively justified
simplifications. Reducing random-intercept variation changes the model and
must be reported as such.


\section{Study lists grouped by observed waves}
\texttt{ctmaInit()} returns two additional study lists and a design overview,
including when \texttt{fit=FALSE}. These preserve the input preparation format,
original study identifiers, order, data and study-level metadata. No study is
excluded from the full fit.
\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
initFit$studyDesign
studyList_two <- initFit$studyLists$twoWaves
studyList_three_plus <- initFit$studyLists$threeOrMoreWaves
# Each nonempty list can be supplied as primaryStudies to ctmaInit().
# This is an optional sensitivity analysis, not an exclusion recommendation.
if (!is.null(studyList_three_plus)) {
  setup_three_plus <- ctmaInit(
    primaryStudies = studyList_three_plus,
    n.latent = 2, fit = FALSE,
    activeDirectory = activeDirectory)
}
\end{lstlisting}

An empty group is \texttt{NULL}. Grouping uses the maximum observed occasions
per person; \texttt{minWaves}, \texttt{maxWaves},
\texttt{nSubjectsTwoWaves} and \texttt{nSubjectsThreeOrMoreWaves} expose
mixed observation counts. Rows with all outcomes missing and repeated records
at the same time are not extra occasions. Studies with fewer than two
observed waves or unknown counts are labelled in the overview and remain in
the full result. Cached Excel workbooks are not copied with stale full-study
contents: an available subset summary is rebuilt in a new workbook.

The interpretation depends on the fitting scope. In \texttt{ctmaInit()}, each
primary study has its own random-intercept covariance, whether requested by
\texttt{indVarying} or \texttt{randomIntercepts}. Unrestricted dynamics and
random-intercept covariance generally cannot be separated from a common
single-lag two-wave design without additional restrictions. Unequal intervals
or model restrictions can change identification; three waves alone are not
a guarantee either.

In \texttt{ctmaFit()}, \texttt{indVarying} requests a common population
covariance across the pooled studies. Two-wave studies are therefore not
subjected to the separate-study warning: identification must be assessed
jointly, using information shared across studies. In contrast,
\texttt{randomIntercepts} requests study-specific covariance matrices unless
explicitly restricted to a pooled covariance. The corresponding two-wave
studies receive a labelled warning, but all studies remain included and no
constraints are changed. The design risk flag in \texttt{studyDesign} refers
to the separate-primary-study model, not to identification of a subsequent
pooled model.


\section{Common drift with study-specific stationary covariance}
With CoTiMA2's native \texttt{backend="ctsemjulia"}, a pooled ML model may
retain common drift while every study has its own stationary within-person
covariance. Saturated dummy and weighted study coding are supported. All
free diffusion covariance coordinates must be allowed to vary across all
study contrasts. These contrasts are moved to the auxiliary stationary
covariance coordinates. The derived diffusion then varies by study as well:
\[
 Q_s=-(A\Sigma_s+\Sigma_s A^\top).
\]
This neither fixes the within-person variances to one nor turns the
population random-intercept covariance into a study-specific covariance.
Stationary initialization uses the same within covariance; there is no
additional independent Time-0 process covariance. The free parameter count
is preserved, but raw covariance contrast coefficients have new meanings.
Old raw starting vectors cannot be reused.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
FullFit_ML2 <- ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = InitFit_ML,
  activeDirectory = activeDirectory,
  backend = "ctsemjulia",
  optimize = TRUE, priors = FALSE,
  indVarying = "MANIFEST", indVaryingT0 = FALSE,
  stationary = TRUE, determinedDiffusions = TRUE,
  studyCoding = "dummy", # "weighted" is also supported
  studyVarying = c("CINT", "DIFFUSION", "T0VAR"),
  coresToUse = 8, scaleTime = 1/48
)
\end{lstlisting}

The example retains the default study-varying families. General moderators
on the process covariance, study-varying drift, random process effects,
partial covariance invariance, binary outcomes, phantom intercept factors
and Bayesian estimation remain outside this supported path. A shared raw
diffusion coordinate cannot simply be replaced by a shared stationary
covariance coordinate: those restrictions generally define different models.
Unsupported restrictions cause an error rather than being silently relaxed.
The common-process \texttt{ctmaOptimizeFit(randomDeterminedDiffusions=TRUE)}
search retains its existing restrictions; this extension concerns direct
pooled \texttt{ctmaFit()} calls.


\section{Long-expression setup error corrected in 0.14.57}
The message \texttt{variable names are limited to 10000 bytes} during
\texttt{.ctModelMatSetup()} can arise before estimation when a generated
covariance expression is too long for R's internal name storage. Version
0.14.57 constructs the same process covariance directly from its marginal
standard deviations and Fisher-transformed correlations, avoiding repeated
expansion of Cholesky expressions. Parameter labels, study contrasts,
random-intercept safeguards and the scientific model are retained. This is
an implementation repair, not a reason to remove studies or alter covariance
constraints. Install the corrected release and rerun the original model
specification. This repair does not require changing the ctsem installation.


\section{CoTiMA2 0.14.58: retired controls and study-order safety}
The \code{experimental} and \code{activateRPB} arguments have been removed;
omit them from older calls, even when their value was \code{FALSE}.
Their former default statistical behavior is retained. Pushbullet
notifications are no longer provided.

\code{ctmaFit(primaryStudyList=...)} now preserves the exact requested
original-study order as well as membership. Initialization fits, cached
data and metadata are matched together. Raw starting values still require
the same model, study order and parameter layout. A saved six-study RI
solution was reproduced by one corrected Stan refit to within approximately
\(4\times10^{-8}\) in \(-2\ell\). Six projected weak directions remained;
this verifies the handoff, not the standard errors or confidence intervals.
Older installed packages do not acquire this correction automatically.


\section*{CoTiMA2 0.14.59: compact initialization notes}
Identification and uncertainty notes now display one row per affected study,
with the recorded weak-direction count, the number of coordinates with
unavailable uncertainty, and flags. The flag explanations are printed once:
T denotes the two-wave design warning, S a saturated transformation, B a
boundary random-effect correlation, and I unstable curvature-based interval
widths. Unknown messages remain visible in full under O. NA means that a
quantity was not recorded in these notes, not that the study is unaffected.
The original full messages and parameter names are retained; use
\code{print(fit$summary$identification_notes, details=TRUE)} or
\code{as.character(fit$summary$identification_notes)} to inspect them.
No study is excluded by this display. During each \code{ctmaInit()} call,
only the first two-wave warning includes the full explanation; later warnings
retain the study label and reference that explanation. Existing saved fits
can be reformatted with \code{summary(fit)} without re-estimation once the
updated package is installed. This development change is not in the original
0.14.58 archive.

\end{document}
